1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:10,752
At ten past three in the afternoon on the thirty-first of May, 1889, an earth dam in the mountains of western Pennsylvania gave way.

2
00:00:12,052 --> 00:00:20,359
Behind it was one of the largest artificial lakes in the United States. In about forty minutes, the entire lake emptied into the narrow valley below.

3
00:00:21,659 --> 00:00:35,125
Fourteen miles downstream was the steel town of Johnstown. When the water reached it, it was no longer really water. It was a moving wall of trees, houses, railway cars, barbed wire and bodies.

4
00:00:36,425 --> 00:00:42,259
In about ten minutes, it destroyed most of the town. Two thousand two hundred and nine people died.

5
00:00:43,559 --> 00:00:54,692
The dam that failed belonged to a private club, whose members included some of the richest men in America. They had used the lake for fishing and sailing. And for years, they had been warned.

6
00:00:57,092 --> 00:01:12,979
The South Fork Dam was built by the State of Pennsylvania between 1838 and 1853, to hold water for a canal system. It was an earthen dam, a huge bank of clay and rock, more than seventy feet high.

7
00:01:14,279 --> 00:01:27,862
But by the time it was finished, railways had made the canal obsolete. The dam was abandoned, sold, and neglected. In 1862, part of it failed, but the lake was low and little damage was done.

8
00:01:29,162 --> 00:01:44,132
In 1879, the dam and the land around it were bought by a group of wealthy men from Pittsburgh. They formed the South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club, and turned the reservoir, now called Lake Conemaugh, into a private summer retreat.

9
00:01:45,432 --> 00:01:58,720
The club's members would come to include the steel magnates Andrew Carnegie and Henry Clay Frick, and the banker Andrew Mellon. They built cottages and a clubhouse by the water, stocked the lake with fish, and sailed on it in summer.

10
00:02:01,120 --> 00:02:12,345
To restore the lake, the club repaired the breached dam. But the work was done cheaply, without a qualified engineer. Holes were filled with mud, rocks, brush and even horse manure.

11
00:02:13,645 --> 00:02:17,511
Over time, three changes made the dam far more dangerous.

12
00:02:18,811 --> 00:02:36,283
The pipes at the base of the dam, which could have been used to lower the lake in an emergency, had been removed and sold. The top of the dam was lowered by several feet, so a road across it would be wide enough for two carriages. And screens were put across the spillway, to stop the club's expensive fish from escaping.

13
00:02:37,583 --> 00:02:49,970
Lowering the crest meant water could flow over the top sooner. The dam had also sagged in the middle, making that part the lowest point. And the screens meant that in a flood, the spillway could clog with debris.

14
00:02:51,270 --> 00:03:07,603
In Johnstown, people knew about the lake above them. Daniel Morrell, head of the town's huge iron works, sent an engineer to inspect the dam. The report was worrying. Morrell offered to help pay for repairs. The club's president rejected the criticism.

15
00:03:08,903 --> 00:03:16,460
In town, the dam became a running joke. Every spring, people said it would break someday. Every spring, it did not.

16
00:03:18,860 --> 00:03:32,890
On the thirtieth of May, 1889, a huge storm moved over western Pennsylvania. Over the next day, between six and ten inches of rain fell, the heaviest rainfall ever recorded in the area.

17
00:03:34,190 --> 00:03:42,172
In Johnstown, the rivers rose and flooded the streets, as they did most springs. People moved their belongings upstairs and waited.

18
00:03:43,472 --> 00:03:51,685
Up at the lake, the water rose about an inch every ten minutes. The spillway, blocked by debris and fish screens, could not carry it away.

19
00:03:52,985 --> 00:04:06,602
On the morning of the thirty-first, the club's young resident engineer, John Parke, saw that the dam might not hold. He organised men to try to dig a new spillway, and to pile earth on top of the dam. It was not enough.

20
00:04:07,902 --> 00:04:20,719
Parke rode to the nearby village of South Fork to raise the alarm. Telegraph messages were sent down the valley to Johnstown. But the warnings had been heard so many times before that many people did not take them seriously.

21
00:04:23,119 --> 00:04:32,900
At around ten past three in the afternoon, water was pouring over the top of the dam. It cut into the earth on the downstream side, and the dam gave way.

22
00:04:34,200 --> 00:04:38,971
About twenty million tons of water rushed into the valley of the Little Conemaugh River.

23
00:04:40,271 --> 00:04:54,561
It swept down the narrow valley, through the villages of South Fork and Mineral Point and East Conemaugh, picking up everything in its path. Houses. Trees. Locomotives. Miles of barbed wire from a wire works.

24
00:04:55,861 --> 00:05:08,626
The wave slowed when it hit obstacles, and then built up behind them and broke through, faster. By the time it reached Johnstown, the front of it was a churning mass of debris that witnesses described as up to forty feet high.

25
00:05:09,926 --> 00:05:15,150
It reached Johnstown at about seven minutes past four, less than an hour after the dam broke.

26
00:05:17,550 --> 00:05:31,869
The people of Johnstown heard it before they saw it, a deep roar that grew louder. Then the wave came through the town. Brick buildings were crushed. Wooden houses were lifted off their foundations and swept away with families still inside.

27
00:05:33,169 --> 00:05:34,551
It took about ten minutes.

28
00:05:35,851 --> 00:05:48,267
At the lower end of town stood a massive stone railway bridge. The debris piled up against its arches, until it formed a dam of wreckage covering about thirty acres, with hundreds of people trapped inside it.

29
00:05:49,567 --> 00:05:59,504
That evening, the pile caught fire, probably from stoves in the wrecked houses. It burned for days. About eighty people who had survived the flood died in the fire.

30
00:06:00,804 --> 00:06:12,929
Two thousand two hundred and nine people were killed. Ninety-nine entire families died together. Three hundred and ninety-six of the dead were children. Many bodies were never identified.

31
00:06:15,329 --> 00:06:27,768
Help came from across the country and the world. Clara Barton arrived with the young American Red Cross. It was the organisation's first major disaster relief effort. She stayed for five months.

32
00:06:29,068 --> 00:06:46,499
Survivors sued the South Fork Fishing and Hunting Club. They did not win a single case. The courts treated the dam failure as an act of God. No member of the club was ever held responsible, and none paid any compensation, though some gave privately to the relief fund.

33
00:06:47,799 --> 00:07:03,814
The outrage helped change American law. Over the following years, more and more courts adopted the principle that whoever keeps something dangerous on their land, like a reservoir, is responsible if it escapes and causes harm, whether or not anyone can prove they were careless.

34
00:07:06,214 --> 00:07:23,243
The storm was extraordinary. But the dam failed because of choices. Cheap repairs. Safety pipes removed. The crest lowered for a road. The spillway blocked for the sake of the fish. And warnings dismissed, by the club and, in the end, by the town itself.

35
00:07:24,543 --> 00:07:33,070
The people who controlled the dam enjoyed the lake. The people who carried the risk lived fourteen miles away, and had no say in how it was maintained.

36
00:07:34,370 --> 00:07:44,568
And there is another lesson in Johnstown, an uncomfortable one. A danger that is familiar, that has been joked about for years, stops feeling like danger at all.

37
00:07:45,868 --> 00:07:57,054
Johnstown was rebuilt. Today, the remains of the South Fork Dam are part of a national memorial, two halves of an earth bank with a gap between them, where the lake used to be.
